   "title": "Before you start: recall",
   "data": {
    "items": [
     {
      "concept": "C0358",
      "prompt": "What is cybersickness (CS)?",
      "answer": "Cybersickness (CS), also known in the literature as visually induced motion sickness (VIMS), is a condition that can affect individuals when they interact with virtual reality (VR) technology and is primarily relevant to virtual reality.",
      "from_lecture": "10"
     },
     {
      "concept": "C2453",
      "prompt": "What is practice effect?",
      "answer": "In a within-subject design, the practice effect is an improvement in task performance from repeated practice; it is also called a confounding variable and confounds the analysis by adding noise to the data, because performance gains may be due to practice rather than to the interface.",
      "from_lecture": "46"
     },
     {
      "concept": "C2472",
      "prompt": "What is p-value?",
      "answer": "The result of a statistical significance test can include the absolute difference in group means, the two-tailed p-value, the T value, the degrees of freedom, and whether the difference is statistically significant, and such results are typically reported, for example, as t = 1.70, p < 0.05, statistically significant, which is the standard way of representing any statistical significance test.",
      "from_lecture": "46"
     },
     {
      "concept": "C2593",
      "prompt": "What is optic flow?",
      "answer": "Optic flow is the speed at which the visual scene changes, and movement is perceived through this change in the view of the surroundings; in a virtual reality (VR) tour, user movement appears as a constantly changing scene.",
      "from_lecture": "49"
     },
     {
      "concept": "C0002",
      "prompt": "What is virtual reality (VR)?",
      "answer": "Virtual reality (VR) is, according to the Miriam Webster dictionary, an artificial environment experienced through sensory stimuli, in which sights and sounds play a primary role and are provided by a computer, possibly along with other sensory stimuli, and in which a person's actions partially determine what happens in the environment.",
      "from_lecture": "4"
     },
     {
      "concept": "C0218",
      "prompt": "What is Oculus Rift?",
      "answer": "Oculus Rift is an inexpensive virtual reality (VR) headset created by Palmer Luckey in 2012.",
      "from_lecture": "6"
     }
    ]
   },
